<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm Outside. I'm Happy! – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Can Connect with Nature!</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Outside. I'm Happy!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe routines and simple procedures. Interactive: Collaborate verbally in team activities.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe outdoor routines (e.g., "I go outside in the afternoon."). Can collaborate with classmates in verbal games or team activities (e.g., "Make two teams. Together, write or draw outdoor activities on the board."). Can describe personal nature experiences to describe abilities or observations in nature (e.g., "I can see trees, but I can't see flowers.").</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 5px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks them to stand up. The teacher shows flashcards of nature vocabulary (tree, flower, bird, park, sun) from previous lessons and elicits choral responses. The teacher says, "Look! It's a tree!" and students repeat. Then the teacher asks, "Are you happy outside?" and encourages a "Yes!" response. The teacher leads a simple TPR activity: "Look up at the sky!" (students look up), "Smell the flowers!" (students pretend to smell). This activates prior knowledge and connects to the theme "I'm Outside. I'm Happy!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models target sentences using flashcards or a nature poster. "I can see a green tree." (pointing to a tree). "I can hear the birds." (mimicking listening). "I am happy in the park." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "sun" by exaggerating the sounds. The teacher blends sounds for words like "sun" (/s/ /ʌ/ /n/) and "flower" (/f/ /l/ /ɑʊ/ /ɚ/), asking students to repeat the blended words. The teacher then models simple routines: "In the morning, I can see the sun. In the afternoon, I walk in the park."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and concepts.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a park) "Is this a house or a park?" (Expected answer: "A park.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a 'can' flashcard) "Can I fly like a bird?" (Expected answer: "No, you can't.") "Can I walk in the park?" (Expected answer: "Yes, you can.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a 'happy' emoji) "Is this a sad face or a happy face?" (Expected answer: "A happy face.")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large poster showing a simple nature scene with various elements (tree, flower, bird, river, sun, grass) and characters engaged in actions (walking, relaxing, looking). Speech bubbles above characters illustrate simple dialogues and descriptions using target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., Character 1: "I can see a bird in the tree. I hear the birds." Character 2: "I like sitting here. It's peaceful." Character 3: "I'm outside. I'm happy!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing connection with nature: Talking about sensory observations and experiences in nature (e.g., "I hear the birds." "The flowers smell nice."). Locating people and things: Providing directions or identifying locations in nature (e.g., "I am near the big tree." "We are at the park entrance.").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher points to different elements in the poster and reads the corresponding speech bubble or description. Students listen and point.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are the people doing?" (Expected: "They are outside.") "Are they happy or sad?" (Expected: "Happy.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher says, "The bird is in the river." (Students say "False!"). "The bird is in the tree." (Students say "True!"). "I can smell the tree." (Students say "False!"). "I can smell the flower." (Students say "True!").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Repeat after me:</b> Teacher models phrases from the speech bubbles, and students repeat in chorus and individually: "I can see a tree." "I'm happy!" "I like this spot."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using "Can/Can't," prepositions of place (in, on, near), and present simple with nature vocabulary (tree, grass, flower, bird, park, walk, see, hear, smell, relax, happy, peaceful).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Sentence Builder" (Whole Class / Choral Speaking):</b> The teacher uses large picture cards or the nature poster. The teacher points to an image and starts a sentence, and students complete it orally. E.g., Teacher: "I can see a..." (points to a flower). Students (choral): "Flower." Teacher: "The bird is..." (points to bird in tree). Students: "In the tree." Teacher: "I can..." (points to ears). Students: "Hear the birds." The teacher reinforces /k/ and /s/ sounds.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "What Can You Do/See?" (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of small nature picture cards (e.g., tree, river, flower, sun, walk, relax). One student picks a card and asks, "What can you see?" or "What can you do?" The other student answers using "I can see/do..." E.g., Student A: (shows 'flower') "What can you see?" Student B: "I can see a beautiful flower. I can smell the flower." Student A: (shows 'walk') "What can you do?" Student B: "I can walk in the park." Students practice polite requests like "Can you pass me the card, please?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction: "Nature Spot Interview":</b> In pairs, students imagine they are in a nature spot. They take turns asking and answering simple questions about what they can see, hear, and feel. Provide sentence frames: "I can see ___." "I can hear ___." "I am ___ (happy/peaceful) here." "Where is the ___?" "It's near/in/on ___." Teacher circulates, listening for accurate use of "can/can't", prepositions, and target vocabulary.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Describe My Nature View"</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a large picture of a different nature scene (e.g., a forest, a river with a waterfall, a park with a picnic). One student in the group is the "Speaker," and the others are "Listeners" and "Drawers." The Speaker describes elements in their picture, using sentences like: "I can see a tall tree. There is a bird in the tree. I can hear the water fall. I can't see any spiders. I'm near the river. It's beautiful and peaceful." The Listeners listen carefully. One Listener can verbally ask questions for clarification (e.g., "Can you see flowers?"). Another Listener attempts to draw what the Speaker describes based *only* on the verbal description, without seeing the picture. After 3-4 minutes, the group compares the drawing to the original picture and discusses (verbally) what is similar or different. Students rotate roles so everyone gets a chance to be the Speaker and Drawer. This activity focuses on fluent description, asking and answering questions, and collaborative verbal interaction.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students actively "connect with nature" by verbally observing and describing elements of different natural environments, and expressing their feelings ("I'm happy! It's peaceful.") as they would if they were truly "outside."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students during the "Describe My Nature View" activity, noting their participation, use of target language, and collaboration. Students also provide simple verbal peer feedback.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students verbally describe a nature scene (from the "Performance" stage) to their group, demonstrating their ability to describe observations and feelings using target vocabulary and grammar. They also interact with questions from peers.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "sun," and blended words.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 5-7 words from the nature vocabulary list (e.g., tree, bird, river, flower, peaceful, beautiful).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of grammar:</b> Accurately uses "I can/can't see...", "It is in/on/near...", "I am happy/peaceful."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and detail:</b> Provides enough verbal information for a peer to understand or draw key elements of the scene.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively participates in group discussion, asks or answers questions politely (e.g., "Can you tell me more?").</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students verbally answer these questions before leaving:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I describe three things I can see in nature?" (e.g., "Yes, I can see a tree, a bird, and a flower.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I tell my friend where something is in nature?" (e.g., "Yes, the bird is in the tree.")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you like about talking about nature today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult when you described your picture to your friends?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students actively participated in the speaking activities, particularly enjoying the "Describe My Nature View" task. The use of picture cards and sentence frames was effective for supporting their verbal output at A1.1. Some students struggled with consistently using prepositions of place, needing more targeted practice. Pronunciation of /k/ and /s/ showed improvement, but blending two-syllable words still needs reinforcement. For the next lesson (Writing), I need to ensure students can translate their spoken sentences into simple written ones, focusing on sentence structure and capitalization. I will provide a sentence starter handout and guided writing exercises to scaffold this transition.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to verbally describe nature scenes and observations. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by guiding students to write simple sentences and short paragraphs describing their favorite nature spot or a nature scene, using the same vocabulary and grammar learned and practiced today.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>